## 5. Venture: the Adventure difficulty

Venture replaces Easy, Medium, Hard and Brutal inside Adventure. It is one difficulty that grows with the level. **The key rule: at Level 1 Venture is easy, and every level it is exactly 1.5× smarter and 1.05× stronger than the level before.** Smarter and stronger grow at different rates on purpose, each from its own constant in `js/data/adventure.js` so they can be tuned separately:

- `VENTURE_SMARTS_GROWTH = 1.5`: the intelligence factor at level L is `1.5 ** (L − 1)`. It is bounded by perfect play (§5.1).
- `VENTURE_STRENGTH_GROWTH = 1.05`: the damage factor at level L is `1.05 ** (L − 1)`. That is ×1 at L1, about ×1.55 at L10, about ×2.53 at L20 and about ×4.12 at L30.

Strength grows more gently because damage is unbounded. At 1.5× per level the damage would reach about ×128,000 by Level 30 and turn the late game into one-hit knockouts. At 1.05× Level 30 is brutal but still winnable, and the Perquisites still matter.

Create `js/data/adventure.js` for all Adventure data: the level count (30), the milestone levels, Venture, and the Perquisite table (§6).

### 5.1 Smarter

CPU Intelligence must stay the same engine with the same capabilities. Venture is a **profile**, the same shape as the profiles in `js/data/difficulty.js`, generated per level by `ventureProfile(level)`:

- **Start point:** Level 1 is the `easy` profile exactly.
- **Each level, every trait's distance from perfect play is divided by `VENTURE_SMARTS_GROWTH` (1.5).** For a trait where lower is better (`react`, `lapse`, `think`, `noise`, `rangeError`, `predictError`, `timing`), compute `value(L) = floor + (easy − floor) / 1.5^(L−1)`. For a trait where higher is better, compute `value(L) = ceil − (ceil − easy) / 1.5^(L−1)`.
- **Bounds:** the floor and ceil are the physical limits of play, not Brutal. Never make reaction zero: keep the react floor at least 2 simulation frames, matching the spirit of "never on the frame it starts". `planDepth` is an integer from 1 to 4, so round it. Probabilities stay within 0–1.
- **Result:** the CPU really is 1.5× closer to perfect every level. By about Level 6 it matches Brutal on most traits, and from there it keeps closing in on perfect play.
- **Ordering:** honour the `CAPABILITY` ordering. No trait may get worse from one level to the next. Write a test for this across all 30 levels.
- **Wiring:** expose the profile through the same path the brain already uses (`getDifficultyProfile`). Let `CombatAIController` and `Brain` accept a `profile` (or `{ venture: level }`) as well as a difficulty id. Do not add a fifth entry to `DIFFICULTIES`: the Difficulty screen and Watch Mode must still list only Easy to Brutal.

### 5.2 Stronger

This is the one deliberate exception to the "no difficulty ever scales a hit" rule in the header of `js/data/difficulty.js`. Update that comment and §7.2.8 of the spec to say so: **only in Adventure**, and only through the battle-level modifier described in §7.

- Venture's fighter deals `damage × ventureStrength(L)`, where `ventureStrength(L) = VENTURE_STRENGTH_GROWTH ** (L − 1)`, on every real (unblocked) hit. That covers its attacks, strikes, projectiles, summons, techniques and backstabs: everything that goes through `CombatSystem.applyHit` with the Venture fighter as the credited attacker, including hits credited through a projectile, summon or technique owner.
- The authored tiers (`ALLOWED_DAMAGE_VALUES`: 1, 3, 5, 10) are still validated on the **definition**. The multiplier applies at **resolution time**, in `applyHit`, to the amount added to the target's Launch Point and Limit gauge. So the launch strength (Base Launch × new Launch Point) grows with it, which is the point.
- Blocked hits still deal 0 and still cost the normal Energy. Shield, Deflect, Energy, speed, cooldowns, hitboxes and stun are untouched. "Stronger" means damage dealt.
- Keep fractional Launch Points internally if a multiplier produces them. The HUD and Match Results display whole numbers (rounded), and the overtime Launch Point tiebreak compares the real values.
- **Show it to the player, honestly:** before the battle, show a brief line on the loading or versus transition, e.g. "Venture · ×11.39 smarter · ×1.22 damage". On the level selector, show it in the wide row.

> Keep `VENTURE_STRENGTH_GROWTH` and the single `ventureStrength(level)` function as the only places to change if strength is retuned later. Do the same for `VENTURE_SMARTS_GROWTH` and `ventureProfile(level)`.

## 6. Perquisites

### 6.1 When the cards appear

- **Before** the battle of levels **5, 10, 15, 20, 25 and 30**, the first time the player starts that level (the milestone is not yet claimed), after Select Fighter.
- Replaying a level whose milestone is already claimed shows no cards.
- **Losing does not unclaim it.** The choice is made when picked, not when the level is won.
- That makes six picks in a full run.

### 6.2 The overlay (new: `js/ui/perquisite-cards.js`)

- Use a modal `role="dialog"` over the dimmed screen, like `ChoiceDialog`. Kicker: "Level NN". Title: "Choose a Perquisite" (French « Choisissez une Perquisite »).
- **Exactly two cards** pop in side by side, with a short scale and fade entrance. Respect reduced motion: no scale, just a fade. On a narrow portrait screen, stack them.
- Each card shows:
  - an original icon, drawn as an SVG in `js/ui/icons.js`
  - the name
  - the level badge in Roman numerals I–V, plus "NEW" when it is level I
  - the effect at that level in plain numbers, e.g. "Take 15% less damage"
  - if it is an upgrade, a muted "was 10%"
- The player **must** pick one. There is no close button, and Esc or Back does nothing on this dialog. Arrow keys and the D-pad move between the cards. Click, Enter/J or gamepad A picks. Hover previews only.
- Picking saves at once (level +1 for that Perquisite, milestone marked claimed), then the battle starts.

### 6.3 Which two cards

- Each of the five Perquisites has five levels (I–V). An offer is always the **next** level of a Perquisite: level I if the player doesn't have it, or one above what they own. Once a Perquisite reaches level V it never appears again.
- **A level the player already owns never comes back.** After picking Brave Soldier I, Brave Soldier I is gone forever, and only Brave Soldier II can be offered.
- Draw the two cards from the Perquisites that are not yet at level V, with a seeded RNG: the run seed plus the milestone level. The two cards are always two **different** Perquisites. Save the drawn pair with the milestone, so reloading the page mid-choice shows the same two cards rather than a reroll.
- With 5 × 5 = 25 levels available and only 6 picks, there is always at least two Perquisites available. Assert this in a test anyway.

### 6.4 The five Perquisites and their scaling

"Every level amplifies the Perquisite by 0.5×": level n's effect is `base × (1 + 0.5 × (n − 1))`. That gives I = ×1, II = ×1.5, III = ×2, IV = ×2.5, V = ×3 the base. Put every base value in `js/data/adventure.js`.

| id | Name (EN / FR) | Effect | Base (I) | II | III | IV | V |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `braveSoldier` | Brave Soldier / Brave Soldat | Damage **received** reduced (the amount added to the player's Launch Point by real hits) | 8% | 12% | 16% | 20% | 24% |
| `nearlyImmortal` | Nearly Immortal / Presque Immortel | Launch Points recovered **per recovery tick** increased (`LAUNCH_RECOVERY_AMOUNT`, normally 2) | +0.5 | +0.75 | +1 | +1.25 | +1.5 |
| `velociousImmuneSystem` | Velocious Immune System / Système Immunitaire Véloce | **Recovery ticks come faster**: `LAUNCH_RECOVERY_INTERVAL` (0.5 s) shortened. This is NOT the 2 s `LAUNCH_RECOVERY_DELAY` before recovery starts, which stays untouched | −8% interval | −12% | −16% | −20% | −24% |
| `maximumEnergy` | Maximum Energy / Énergie Maximale | Energy **cost** of everything that costs Energy reduced (Dash, air dash, Shield block, Deflect, constructs, any move's own cost) | 8% | 12% | 16% | 20% | 24% |
| `redoutableStrength` | Redoutable Strength / Force Redoutable | Damage **dealt** increased (on real hits, the same resolution point as Venture's strength) | 8% | 12% | 16% | 20% | 24% |

Fix the spellings in the UI: "Brave Soldier", "Maximum Energy", "Redoutable Strength" and "Velocious Immune System". The ids above are final.

Each card states its effect in plain words and real numbers. For example, for Velocious Immune System: "Launch Point recovers every 0.44 s instead of 0.5 s (recovery still starts after 2 s)".

## 7. How modifiers reach the fighters (architecture)

- Add a per-fighter **`modifiers`** object to the Battle, built once at battle creation. Default to identity for every mode: `damageDealt: 1, damageTaken: 1, recoveryAmountBonus: 0, recoveryIntervalScale: 1, energyCostScale: 1`.
  - Only Adventure builds non-identity values: the player's from their Perquisites, and the CPU's from Venture's strength.
  - Quick Battle, Watch, Local, Online and Practice must be byte-for-byte the same in behaviour. Online lockstep must not change at all, and the state hash must be unaffected when modifiers are identity.
- **Never** read a fighter id, a mode string or Adventure state inside the combat engine. The engine reads only the modifiers object it is handed (`fighter.combat.modifiers` or similar). That keeps the character-system rule intact.
- **Hooks:**
  - `CombatSystem.applyHit`: `damage = base × attacker.damageDealt × target.damageTaken`, applied to the Launch Point increase and to `fillLimit`. A hit with a base of 0 stays 0.
  - `CombatState.updateLaunchRecovery`: amount `LAUNCH_RECOVERY_AMOUNT + recoveryAmountBonus`, interval `LAUNCH_RECOVERY_INTERVAL × recoveryIntervalScale`. The delay is unchanged.
  - `spendEnergy` callers, or better the fighter's `energySpec` built per battle: every cost × `energyCostScale`.
- **CPUs have no Perquisites.** The Venture CPU's modifiers are identity except `damageDealt`.
- **Match statistics** (`js/game/match-stats.js`) report the real, modified numbers.

## 8. CPUs must acknowledge this update

The CPU Intelligence must reason with the **real, modified** numbers in Adventure, not the authored ones. Otherwise its finisher, risk and objective reasoning will be wrong. Audit every place the brain predicts damage, launch strength, recovery or Energy. Start with `evaluator.js`, `situation.js`, `objective.js`, `strategy.js`, `plans.js`, `defense.js`, `values.js` and `opponent-model.js`. Route those predictions through helpers that take the modifiers, e.g. `effectiveDamage(attacker, target, def)`, `effectiveRecovery(fighter)` and `effectiveEnergyCost(fighter, cost)`. Specifically:

- **Its own hits:** it knows each hit lands `× damageDealt`, so it recognises a kill-confirm at the level-scaled launch strength. At high levels it should go for finishers much earlier.
- **The player's Perquisites:**
  - Against Brave Soldier, it knows its hits do less and the player survives longer.
  - Against Nearly Immortal and Velocious Immune System, it knows the player's Launch Point drains faster, so pressure that lets up is worth less. It should keep momentum instead of waiting.
  - Against Redoutable Strength, it respects the player's hits more in its risk and guard weighting.
  - Against Maximum Energy, its read of "the opponent is about to run dry" uses the player's reduced costs.
- **Its own Energy reads** still use full costs, since it has no Perquisites.
- **Overtime and the tiebreak:** its awareness of the Launch Point tiebreak uses the real, possibly fractional, values.
- **The CPU's perception rules are unchanged:** it still sees only what has been on screen for its reaction window. The modifiers are known rules of the match, like the clock, not perception.
- **Celebration AI** (`js/game/ai/celebration.js`) and the Watch Mode CPUs are unaffected.
- **Tests (in `tests/systems/`):**
  - With identity modifiers the brain's decisions are identical to before, using the same seeded scenario.
  - With `damageDealt` raised, its evaluated finisher threshold drops.
  - With the player's `damageTaken` lowered, the brain predicts the lower Launch Point.

